(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physical-diagnosis-1-exam-3-pd1-exam3-master-exam-form-a"]={"title":"PD1 Exam 3 Comprehensive Master Exam — Form A","category":"Physical Diagnosis","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"How is the sensory function of the trigeminal nerve tested?","choices":["By asking the patient to smell coffee","By having the patient say the word Ah","By testing light touch in all three facial divisions","By checking the patient's grip strength"],"answer":2,"exp":"Trigeminal sensory function is tested by applying light touch (and pain) with a cotton wisp or swab in all three divisions — ophthalmic, maxillary, and mandibular. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 25"},{"q":"Cranial nerve one, the olfactory nerve, is tested by assessing what?","choices":["The sense of vision","The sense of smell","The sense of hearing","The sense of taste"],"answer":1,"exp":"Cranial nerve one, the olfactory nerve, is a sensory nerve tested by assessing the sense of smell in each nostril with a familiar aroma such as coffee or cinnamon. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 20"},{"q":"What structures are specifically palpated during the rectovaginal exam?","choices":["The rectovaginal septum and the posterior fornix","Only the labia majora, assessed externally","Only the urethral meatus and its opening","Only the anterior fornix, near the bladder"],"answer":0,"exp":"The rectovaginal exam specifically palpates the rectovaginal septum and posterior fornix. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 44"},{"q":"Cervical lateral bending, bringing the ear toward the shoulder, occurs mainly at which levels?","choices":["The skull and C1 only","The C2 through C7 levels","The C1 to C2 level only","The thoracic T4 to T8 levels"],"answer":1,"exp":"Cervical lateral bending, bringing the ear toward the shoulder (normally about 40 degrees), occurs mainly at the C2 through C7 levels. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 24"},{"q":"Your patient with painful urination has no fever, chills, flank pain, costovertebral angle tenderness, abdominal pain, nausea or vomiting. What does that combination of pertinent negatives let you argue in the differential?","choices":["That an ascending infection such as pyelonephritis is less likely","That a lower urinary tract infection can be excluded outright","That no infection of any kind is present","That imaging is required before any diagnosis can be offered"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pertinent negatives argue against alternatives; those findings are the signs of ascending infection. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"You are writing the follow-up line of a plan for an uncomplicated infection. What does the lecture require it to contain?","choices":["A fixed appointment six months later, regardless of progress","No follow-up, since the prescription has been issued","Follow-up only once the culture sensitivities have been reported","A stated interval to return if symptoms have not resolved, plus an instruction to call sooner if anything worsens"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A follow-up line gives both a defined interval to return and a safety net for new, worsening or otherwise concerning symptoms. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 49"},{"q":"To palpate the temporomandibular joint, where does the examiner place the fingertips?","choices":["Just in front of the tragus of each ear","Over the mastoid process behind each ear","At the angle of the mandible near the chin","Along the midline of the hard palate"],"answer":0,"exp":"To assess the temporomandibular joint, the examiner places the fingertips just in front of the tragus of each ear and has the patient open and close the mouth. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 20"},{"q":"What does the lecture say the differential statements should achieve for a reader?","choices":["They should list every condition capable of affecting the organ in question","They should record the exact wording the patient used when describing it","Other providers should be able to follow your thought process from them","They should establish which of the tests the insurer is willing to reimburse"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states this directly as the purpose of the differential statements. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"What belongs in the subjective portion of a progress note?","choices":["A complete retelling of the original history of present illness","Vital signs and updated laboratory results","Updates on patient comments or complaints since the previous note","The final diagnosis with supporting argument"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture describes updates on comments or complaints — changes, pain level, symptoms better, worse, resolved, or new — covering the interval since the last note. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"What is the first step of the rectovaginal exam, regarding gloves?","choices":["Use the same glove from the bimanual exam without changing it","Remove the outer glove, since there should be a second glove underneath","Apply a fresh third glove layer directly over the existing two gloves","No glove change is needed between bimanual and rectovaginal exam"],"answer":1,"exp":"The rectovaginal exam begins by removing the outer (now-contaminated vaginal) glove, revealing a clean second glove underneath. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 44"},{"q":"What thickening finding on breast inspection is concerning?","choices":["Thickening is only relevant during pregnancy","Thickening suggestive of breast cancer","Thickening only matters if bilateral","Thickening is always a normal, benign finding"],"answer":1,"exp":"Thickening on inspection is a concerning finding suggestive of breast cancer. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 29"},{"q":"What shape does the external cervical os typically have in a nulliparous woman versus after childbirth?","choices":["Always slit-like regardless of childbirth history","Slit-like in the nulliparous woman; round after childbirth","Always round regardless of childbirth history","Round in the nulliparous woman; slit-like after childbirth"],"answer":3,"exp":"The external os is typically round before childbirth and becomes slit-like after vaginal delivery. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 15"},{"q":"Evaluating a patient for aphasia is part of assessing which mental status component?","choices":["Muscle tone","Coordination","Pupillary reflex","Language"],"answer":3,"exp":"Evaluating for aphasia assesses language, including word comprehension, repetition, naming, reading comprehension, and writing. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 14"},{"q":"How are Skene's glands assessed if needed?","choices":["They must be \"milked\" from the interior","They are visualized directly with the speculum","They are assessed only via ultrasound","They cannot be assessed on physical exam"],"answer":0,"exp":"Skene's glands are assessed by milking them from the interior if needed, given their periurethral location. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 26"},{"q":"What is the Pouch of Douglas, and how is it clinically accessible?","choices":["The external cervical os, a small opening reachable only on direct visual inspection with a speculum","A pocket of the vaginal wall that surrounds the cervix, reachable directly on speculum examination","The fan-like fimbriated end of the fallopian tube, reachable only on bimanual palpation deep in the adnexa","The retrouterine pouch/cul-de-sac formed by peritoneum extending from the uterus onto the rectum, reachable on rectal exam"],"answer":3,"exp":"The Pouch of Douglas (retrouterine pouch) is a peritoneal cul-de-sac between the uterus and rectum, accessible on rectal examination. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 17"},{"q":"The rotator cuff muscles are remembered by the mnemonic 'SITS.' Which muscles does it represent?","choices":["Sternocleidomastoid, iliopsoas, trapezius, soleus","Supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres minor, subscapularis","Supinator, interossei, triceps, sartorius","Subclavius, intercostals, teres major, serratus"],"answer":1,"exp":"The rotator cuff mnemonic SITS stands for the supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres minor, and subscapularis. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 32"},{"q":"How does the lecture describe the review of systems?","choices":["A focused enquiry limited to the system named in the chief complaint","A head-to-toe survey of body systems and symptoms, covering each system in full detail","A restatement of the physical examination findings in the patient's words","A summary of the patient's previous specialist consultations"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. That is the lecture's description. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"How does the lecture describe the physical examination within the objective portion of a progress note?","choices":["A full head-to-toe examination repeated identically each day","Omitted entirely, on the basis that it was already documented in the history and physical","Limited strictly to the vital signs","Focused and detailed, covering systems pertinent to the complaint and positive history"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture calls for a focused, detailed examination of systems pertinent to the chief complaint and positive history, alongside updated vitals and labs. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"What do the four letters of the SOAP note stand for?","choices":["Symptoms, observations, analysis, prescription","Subjective, objective, assessment, plan","Source, onset, aggravators, palliators","Screening, orders, admission, procedure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture gives these four sections and calls the note a standardized way to document encounters. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"In the OLD ChARTS mnemonic, what do the letters C and A stand for?","choices":["Chronology and any associated illness","Character and alleviating or aggravating factors","Cause and associated abdominal findings","Comorbidities and known allergies"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. OLD ChARTS runs onset, location, duration, character, alleviating and aggravating factors, radiation, temporal pattern, and symptoms associated. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Interpreting a proverb such as 'you can't judge a book by its cover' tests which mental status function?","choices":["Abstract reasoning","Peripheral vibratory sense","Deep tendon reflex activity","Hand grip strength testing"],"answer":0,"exp":"Interpreting proverbs and solving problems tests abstract reasoning during the mental status exam. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 15"},{"q":"Between which ribs does the breast lie?","choices":["The 3rd and 8th rib","The 1st and 3rd rib","The 2nd and 6th rib","The 5th and 9th rib"],"answer":2,"exp":"The breast lies between the 2nd and 6th rib, spanning the sternal edge to the midaxillary line. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 4"},{"q":"Which structures connect muscle to bone?","choices":["Ligaments","Tendons","Bursae","Synovium"],"answer":1,"exp":"Tendons are inelastic strong fibrous collagen fibers that connect muscle to bone. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 3"},{"q":"What equipment is needed for a Pap smear as part of the pelvic exam?","choices":["A tuning fork and an otoscope for ear and hearing tests","Only a speculum and a small tube of lubricant gel","Cotton swab, spatula, brush, glass slide, and fixative spray","Only a cotton swab and a single glass microscope slide"],"answer":2,"exp":"Pap smear equipment includes a cotton swab, spatula, brush, glass slide, and fixative spray. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 21"},{"q":"Before deciding what to examine, which question does the lecture want you to ask yourself?","choices":["Which findings are most likely to be normal?","What needs examining to rule the worst-case scenario in or out?","What will take the least time to document?","What did the last clinician examine at the previous visit?"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The paired question is what your differentials are and how you can rule them in or out. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What are the labia minora?","choices":["Rounded folds of adipose and connective tissue extending from the mons to past the posterior commissure","Hair-covered fat pads overlying the symphysis pubis","Thinner, pink-red folds that extend anteriorly to form the prepuce","A fossa that lies medial to the labia minora"],"answer":2,"exp":"The labia minora are thin, pink-red folds that extend anteriorly to form the clitoral prepuce. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 8"},{"q":"According to the lecture, how do you decide what counts as pertinent in the subjective section?","choices":["Anything the patient volunteers is pertinent by definition","It depends on the individual case","Only findings that appear in the chief complaint are pertinent","Pertinence is fixed by the note template for that visit type"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture answers its own question with “it depends on the individual case” and then gives categories to consider. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What is the suspensory ligament of the breast called, and what does it connect?","choices":["Stensen's ligament, connecting glandular lobes","Wharton's ligament, connecting the nipple to the areola","Poupart's ligament, connecting the breast to the sternum","Cooper's ligament, connecting skin to breast fascia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Cooper's ligament is the fibrous suspensory ligament connecting the skin to the breast fascia. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 6"},{"q":"A patient presents with right-sided facial pain and nasal congestion. Applying the lecture's rule, how far should your examination reach?","choices":["Every system in full detail, so that nothing at all is missed","Only whatever can be examined without any special equipment","A system above and below the affected one, plus cardiac and respiratory","The nose alone, since that is where the complaint is centred"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture's rule when in doubt is to go a system up and down, and all examinations generally include cardiac and respiratory. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"Which two systems does the lecture say all examinations generally include?","choices":["Neurological and musculoskeletal","Skin and lymphatic","Cardiac and respiratory","Abdominal and genitourinary"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states that all examinations generally include cardiac and respiratory. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"What does the lecture require for a pharmacologic entry in the plan?","choices":["The drug class and an alternative agent if the first fails","A prescription number and the dispensing pharmacy","Only the drug name, since dosing is set by the pharmacist","Indications, drug name, dosage, route, and frequency"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists these, and its worked example gives drug, dose, route, frequency, duration, and indication together. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"Cervical forward flexion, bringing the chin to the chest, is normally about how many degrees?","choices":["About 90 degrees","About 110 degrees","About 45 degrees","About 160 degrees"],"answer":2,"exp":"Cervical forward flexion, bringing the chin to the chest, is normally about 45 degrees and occurs at the skull and C1 (atlas). — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which cervical range-of-motion maneuver is normally about 70 degrees and occurs mainly at the C1-C2 level?","choices":["Forward flexion — chin to chest","Rotation — looking over each shoulder","Hyperextension — looking at the ceiling","Lateral bending — ear to shoulder"],"answer":1,"exp":"Cervical rotation, looking over each shoulder, is normally about 70 degrees and occurs primarily at the C1-C2 (atlas-axis) level. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 24"},{"q":"Pain and temperature sensation are carried primarily by which pathway?","choices":["The spinothalamic tracts","The posterior columns","The corticospinal tracts","The corticobulbar tract"],"answer":0,"exp":"Pain and temperature sensations are carried primarily by the spinothalamic tracts. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 63"},{"q":"Where is the nipple and areola located relative to the breast?","choices":["At the most superior edge of the breast, near the clavicle","Location varies randomly with no consistent anatomic position","At the most medial edge of the breast, near the sternum","At the lateral midline of the breast"],"answer":3,"exp":"The nipple and areola sit at the lateral midline of the breast. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 4"},{"q":"Which problem does the lecture place under content issues rather than technical issues?","choices":["Word errors within an otherwise complete sentence","Inconsistency between two parts of the same note","Vague details such as calling a finding normal or appropriate","Information placed in the wrong section of the document"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Content issues cover not enough information, confusion between a history and physical and a progress note, vague terms such as normal, abnormal or appropriate, and incomplete descriptions of exam findings. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"Touching both sides of the body simultaneously and asking the patient whether they feel one or two touches tests what?","choices":["Extinction (double simultaneous stimulation)","Vibratory sense with a tuning fork","The plantar (Babinski) response","Rapid alternating movements"],"answer":0,"exp":"Extinction is tested by touching each side individually and then both simultaneously; the patient should perceive both — this is double simultaneous stimulation. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 79"},{"q":"What general awareness should the examiner keep in mind throughout the breast exam?","choices":["Sensitivity to the patient's feelings only matters if she explicitly reports discomfort or pain","Only physical positioning and draping matter, not the patient's emotional comfort during the exam","No particular sensitivity is needed, since the exam involves no specialized equipment or instruments","Be alert to the female patient's feelings, given the sensitive nature of the exam"],"answer":3,"exp":"The lecture specifically notes the examiner should be sensitive to the patient's feelings throughout this exam. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 27"},{"q":"What does the acronym structure of the progress note stand for, and how does it compare to a full history and physical?","choices":["Symptoms, observations, analysis, prescription; broader than a history and physical","Subjective, objective, assessment, plan; more focused than a history and physical","Subjective, objective, assessment, plan; identical in scope to a history and physical","Source, onset, aggravators, palliators; used only in outpatient clinics"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture describes the progress note as a standardized way to document encounters, usually for daily evaluation of hospitalized inpatients, and more focused than a full history and physical. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"A grinding, crunching, or crackling felt or heard during joint movement is called what?","choices":["Effusion","Fasciculation","Crepitus","Atrophy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Crepitus is a crunching, crackling, or grinding sensation heard or felt during joint movement, noted during range-of-motion testing. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 15"},{"q":"You are presenting a patient you are seeing for the first time. How much does the lecture say to include?","choices":["Only the medications and allergies","Only what has changed since the last evaluation","The chief complaint and active problems only","The complete medical history"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. New patients get the complete history; established patients in clinic get the chief complaint and active problems. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What is the tail of Spence?","choices":["The nipple's lactiferous duct opening","A pigmented area around the nipple","A breast segment that extends into the axilla","A sebaceous gland elevation on the areola"],"answer":2,"exp":"The tail of Spence is the portion of breast tissue that extends into the axilla. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 20"},{"q":"What color change on inspection may suggest cancer or an inflammatory condition?","choices":["Redness","Yellow discoloration","Pallor","Cyanosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Red coloration on breast inspection may suggest cancer or an inflammatory condition. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 29"},{"q":"What does the lecture say the social history should convey overall?","choices":["Who your patient is and what risk factors they have","A chronological account of every previous hospital admission","The patient's insurance status and ability to pay","A list of relatives with heritable conditions"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture closes the social history slide with exactly this, after listing substance use, diet, exercise, occupation, travel, sexual history, support systems, stresses, safety, and life experiences. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Normal knee flexion is approximately how many degrees?","choices":["About 45 degrees","About 75 degrees","About 130 degrees","About 180 degrees"],"answer":2,"exp":"Normal knee flexion is approximately 130 degrees, with extension from about 0 to 15 degrees. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 59"},{"q":"What are Montgomery tubercles?","choices":["Small, rounded elevations on the areolar surface marking sebaceous glands","The four anatomic quadrants used to describe breast tissue location","Lactiferous ducts that open directly at the tip of the nipple","The tail of Spence, a wedge of breast tissue extending into the axilla"],"answer":0,"exp":"Montgomery tubercles (glandulae areolares) are small rounded elevations on the areola marking sebaceous glands. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 20"},{"q":"Normal hip extension, lifting the leg behind the body, is approximately how many degrees?","choices":["About 90 degrees","About 130 degrees","About 30 degrees","About 5 degrees"],"answer":2,"exp":"Normal hip extension, lifting the leg behind the body, is approximately 30 degrees. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 54"},{"q":"What structures are identified after separating the labia minora?","choices":["Clitoris, urethral meatus, and introitus","Only the ovaries, deep within the pelvis","Only the cervix, visualized with a speculum","Only the fallopian tubes and their fimbriae"],"answer":0,"exp":"Separating the labia minora exposes the clitoris, urethral meatus, and vaginal introitus for inspection. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 25"},{"q":"What nipple/skin condition is associated with rashes or discharge on breast inspection?","choices":["Nipple eczema","Paget's disease","Areolar rosacea","Nipple psoriasis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Paget's disease is the nipple/skin condition associated with rash and discharge findings on breast inspection. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 30"},{"q":"What distinguishes active range of motion (active ROM) from passive range of motion?","choices":["In active ROM the examiner moves the joint for the patient","In active ROM the joint is imaged rather than moved","In active ROM the patient performs the movement themselves","In active ROM the joint is held completely still"],"answer":2,"exp":"In active range of motion, the patient actively performs the movement, whereas in passive range of motion the examiner moves the joint. — Musculoskeletal Physical Examination lecture, Slide 15"},{"q":"How does the lecture qualify the severity element of OPPQRST?","choices":["Record it only when the patient volunteers a number unprompted","Record it as mild, moderate, or severe rather than as a number","Rate it from 1 to 10, and establish what a 10 means for that patient","Rate it from 1 to 5, since finer gradations are not reproducible"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture writes severity as “1-10, what is a 10?”, anchoring the scale to the individual patient. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"How is pronator drift tested?","choices":["The patient rapidly taps the foot on the floor","The patient walks placing heel to toe","The patient squeezes the examiner's fingers","The patient extends both arms forward, palms up, and closes the eyes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Pronator drift is tested by having the patient extend both arms forward with palms up and close the eyes; a downward drift or pronation of one arm is abnormal. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 87"},{"q":"According to Tanner staging of pubarche, what characterizes Stage 3?","choices":["Sparse growth of long, slightly pigmented hair at the base of the labia","Prepubertal, with only vellus hair similar to the abdominal wall","Adult in type and quantity, spread with a horizontal upper border","Darker, coarser, more curled hair spreading sparsely over the junction of the pubes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Stage 3 pubarche is characterized by darker, coarser, curlier hair spreading sparsely over the pubic junction. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 23"},{"q":"Recognizing a familiar object, such as a coin, placed in the hand with the eyes closed tests which discriminative sensation?","choices":["Graphesthesia","Two-point discrimination","Point localization","Stereognosis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Stereognosis is the ability to recognize a familiar object by touch and manipulation alone with the eyes closed, a higher function of the sensory cortex. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 75"},{"q":"How is the uterine body normally positioned relative to the vaginal wall and bladder?","choices":["Almost at a right angle with the vaginal wall, inclined slightly forward over the bladder","Nearly parallel to the vaginal wall, inclined backward and downward over the rectum","There is no typical positional relationship, since uterine position varies randomly","Directly posterior to the rectum, angled sharply away from the bladder"],"answer":0,"exp":"The uterine body sits almost at a right angle to the vaginal wall and is inclined slightly forward (anteverted) over the bladder. — Examination of the Female Genitalia lecture, Slide 14"},{"q":"For which setting does the lecture say progress notes are usually used?","choices":["Daily evaluation of hospitalized inpatients","Initial evaluation of a new outpatient referral","Emergency department triage before a clinician review","Annual preventive visits in primary care"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture describes the progress note as a standardized way to document encounters, usually for daily evaluation of hospitalized inpatients. — PAJ 5300 History and Physical mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"You list four possible diagnoses in the differential but write nothing after each one. What has the lecture asked for that is missing?","choices":["The name of the specialist who would go on to confirm each one","A numerical probability for each possibility","A brief statement tying the patient's findings to each possibility","A published literature citation supporting each possibility"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The most likely comes first with supporting evidence; the others explain why they were considered and why they are less likely. — PAJ 5300 SOAP and Case Presentations mbf2026sc.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"The triceps deep tendon reflex tests primarily which nerve roots?","choices":["C5 and C6","C6 and C7","L2 and L3","S1 and S2"],"answer":1,"exp":"The triceps deep tendon reflex tests primarily the C6 and C7 nerve roots. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 50"},{"q":"Recognizing a number drawn on the palm with the eyes closed tests which discriminative sensation?","choices":["Graphesthesia","Stereognosis","Extinction","Proprioception"],"answer":0,"exp":"Graphesthesia is the ability to identify a number drawn on the skin, such as the palm, with the eyes closed. — Neurological Assessment: CNS and PNS lecture, Slide 76"},{"q":"For patients with large or pendulous breasts, what additional inspection maneuver is recommended?","choices":["Crossing the arms over the chest","Leaning forward","Lying prone","No modification is needed regardless of breast size"],"answer":1,"exp":"Leaning forward is specifically recommended for inspection of large or pendulous breasts. — Physical Examination of the Breasts lecture, Slide 28"}]};
